{{also|utilisé}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|utilize}} {{qualifier|American, Canadian, Oxford British spelling}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|fr|utiliser}}, from {{der|en|it|utilizzare}}, from {{m|it|utile||useful}}, from {{der|en|la|ūtilis}}, from {{m|la|ūtī||use}}. Attested c. 1810.<ref>{{R:Dictionary.com}}</ref>

===Verb===
{{en-verb|utilis}}

# To make {{l|en|use}} of; to [[use]].
# To make [[useful]]; to find a practical use for.
# To make [[best]] use of; to use to its fullest extent, potential, or ability.
# To [[make do]] with; to use in manner different from that originally [[intended]]<ref name="getting-the-words-right">T.A.R. Cheney, ''Getting the Words Right'', Writer's Digest Books (1983).</ref>

====Usage notes====
Many style guides have advised against {{m|en|utilize}} and {{m|en||utilise}}, arguing that the simpler verb {{m|en|use}} is always preferable (and analogously, that the noun {{m|en|use}} is preferable to {{m|en|utilization}} and {{m|en|utilisation}}).<ref>Sir Ernest Gowers 1965 ''The Complete Plain Words'' Oxford: Oxford University Press</ref><ref>Eric Partridge 1973 ''Usage and Abusage: A Guide to Good English'' England: Penguin Books</ref><ref>John E. Kahn (ed) 1985 ''The Right Word at the Right Time'' London:Readers Digest</ref><ref>Pam Peters 1995 ''The Cambridge Australian English Style Guide'' Cambridge:Cambridge University Press</ref> When used simply as a synonym in ordinary writing (as in “please {{m|en||utilise}} the rear door when exiting the aircraft”) it can strike readers as pretentious, and so should be used sparingly.<ref>New Oxford American Dictionary 3rd edition (c) 2010 by Oxford University Press, Inc.</ref> American novelist {{w|David Foster Wallace}} calls it a puff word.<ref>He continues: "Since it does nothing that good old [[use]] doesn't do, its extra letters and syllables don't make a writer seem smarter. I tell my students that using [[utilize]] makes you seem either pompous or so insecure that you'll use pointlessly big words in an attempt to look smart."</ref> Op-ed editor of The Los Angeles Time Juliet Lapidos "There are many bad words in English, but only one worst word. That word is [[utilize]]" <ref>Juliet Lapidos 2017 ''Utilize is the worst word in the English language'' [https://theoutline.com/post/2151/utilize-is-the-worst-word-in-the-english-language]</ref>

It does not follow that because some speakers eschew a particular usage, it must be everywhere redundant. {{m|en||Utilise}} is suited to senses in which {{m|en||use}} would require circumlocution. Examples of such senses include “put to use”, as in “...utilise the production capacities of the local industries fully before ordering from foreign industries.” “exploit or consume”, as in “...utilise the support that the system provides, such as by making the most of tax exemptions and special supplies.” or “make best use of” (profitable, practical use, not just general use), as in “...farmers must {{m|en||utilise}} their land fully to boost food security”. Further, in American usage, {{m|en||utilize}}<!-- z b/c American --> can imply use outside an object’s intended purpose, as in “...our airmen {{m|en||utilized}} damaged drop tanks in the field, cutting them open for bathtubs”.<ref>A story of tanks[https://web.archive.org/web/20170704020923/http://www.avialogs.com/index.php/avialogs/a-story-of-tanks.html]</ref><ref>{{R:Dictionary.com|use}}</ref><ref>{{R:American Heritage 2000}}</ref>

